# a-list-a-mix-would-soften — the LIVENESS control for the pair
# `a-list-of-one-symbol` / `a-list-that-is-mixed`.
#
# ⚠⚠ **Those two read 452 AGAINST 452, and an identical pair is exactly
# what this repo has been wrong about three times in one day** — a
# saturated siege front, a wallet emptied by buying the thing being
# measured, and a fixture that died before its own content arrived.
# `CLAUDE.md`: *a gate that reads PERFECT is as suspect as one that
# reads wrong, and the missing control is generic — CAN this gate
# produce a non-trivial reading at all?*
#
# ⚠ This file is that control.  Same base, same counts, same poke — a
# composition built from SCOUTS instead of builders.  It must read
# clearly softer than 452, and it does: **515**.
#
# ⚠⚠ So the shipped list's 452-against-452 is a real claim about the
# composition and not a fixture that stopped measuring.  ⚠ It is also
# the evidence for `@M090`'s rule in one file: *a wave is decided by the
# worst class holding a slot in the front*, so a scout costs where a
# builder is free.

echo a mix that would soften: the same counts, built from scouts

do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 20 0
marker 20 0 spawn 3
count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -4 -3 22 -3
drag -4 -2 22 -2
drag -4 -1 22 -1
drag -4 0 22 0
drag -4 1 22 1
drag -4 2 22 2
drag -4 3 22 3

# A wall to chew, and a tower behind it — the difference between the
# classes is a WALL-DAMAGE rate and a SPEED, so a base with nothing to
# chew could not tell them apart at all.
# ⚠ A GATE at (6, 0) — the wall is painted around it — so the player can
# drive out to poke the marker and back.  `plans/12` B7 measured that a
# wall with a gate in it buys ONE tick over a sealed one, and it is
# identical in both halves of this pair either way.
palette 9
click 6 -3
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
click 6 3

# ⚠⚠ **Three towers and two crew, and the base needs every one of
# them**: the design's mix starts at WAVE 2, so a base that cannot reach
# wave 2 cannot tell the two halves of this pair apart at all.  The
# first version of this fixture had one tower, fell at 175 having sent
# two waves, and read **175 against 175** — a flat pair measuring a base
# that died before the content it was measuring arrived.
do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
click 3 -2
marker 3 -2 tower
click 1 0
marker 1 0 tower
click 3 2
marker 3 2 tower
do toggle_mode
top 3 -2 on
top 1 0 on
top 3 2 on

# ⚠ The crew repair the towers between waves — `plans/17` T1 measured
# that without upkeep more towers buy NOTHING (3, 5 and 7 towers give
# 321, 319 and 317 ticks and four waves each).
crew 2 -1
crew 2 1
park 0 0

# ⚠⚠ **THE ONE LINE.**  A `schedule` line is a bare `vector<integer>`,
# and `wave_schedule_of` has always meant *N waves of N counts, every
# robot a regular* — so authoring the design's own counts here is how a
# fixture asks for the list WITHOUT its composition.
schedule 5 8 12 20 30 50 80

# ⚠⚠ **THE CONTROL'S ONE JOB: be a composition that DOES soften.**  The
# same counts with scouts in them — 8 robot + 4 scout where the shipped
# file has 8 robot + 4 builder.
compose 2 8 robot 4 scout
compose 3 12 robot 8 scout
compose 4 18 robot 12 scout
compose 5 28 robot 22 scout
compose 6 44 robot 36 scout

# ── The poke ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ The wave list does not start until the player drives out and touches
# a spawn marker 12+ hexes from the core (`WAVE_1_PROVOCATION_HEXES`),
# so both files start their clock the same way and at the same tick.
waves 0 0
drive 20 0
tick 14
vehicle 20 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 14
vehicle 0 0



# same counts, same poke, same ticks — one `schedule` line apart.  The
# finding and its attribution are in the sibling's own tail.
fall 900
ticks 511 519
